INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — Dilution test ordering: rank by incremental EPS, stop when antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A financial reporting team must determine which potentially dilutive securities to include in diluted EPS. Three instruments produce incremental per-share amounts of $0.15, $0.90, and $0.55 respectively. The team proposes testing them in order of highest to lowest incremental amount. Under ASC 260-10-45-21, is this ordering correct?', '["Yes — starting with the highest per-share impact ensures the most conservative result", "No — securities must be ranked from lowest to highest incremental EPS so that the most dilutive are tested first, and testing stops when the next security would increase the cumulative per-share amount", "Yes — the ordering does not matter because all dilutive securities are ultimately included", "No — securities should be tested alphabetically by instrument type for auditability"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 260-10-45-21 requires securities to be ranked from most dilutive (lowest incremental EPS) to least dilutive. The correct order is $0.15, $0.55, $0.90. Testing in this sequence ensures that if a later security causes cumulative diluted EPS to rise (become antidilutive in aggregate), it and all subsequent securities are excluded. Choice A would test the least dilutive first, potentially including it before discovering its aggregate antidilutive effect.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — When basic and diluted EPS are identical, both must still appear
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity with no convertible instruments, options, or warrants asks its auditor whether it may omit the diluted EPS line from the income statement since the figure would equal basic EPS. What guidance does ASC 260-10-45-10 provide?', '["Omission is permitted because reporting identical figures serves no informational purpose", "Both basic and diluted EPS must be presented with equal prominence on the face of the income statement even when the amounts are the same", "Only diluted EPS needs to appear; basic is optional in a simple capital structure", "The entity may combine the two into a single line labeled ''basic/diluted EPS''"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 260-10-45-10 requires dual presentation of basic and diluted EPS with equal prominence regardless of whether they differ. Presenting both confirms to financial statement users that no dilutive securities exist. Choice A incorrectly allows omission, which would leave users unable to confirm the absence of dilutive instruments. Choice D is not a presentation format recognized by the standard.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Contingently issuable shares: price target unmet at year-end
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An acquisition agreement requires the acquirer to issue 40,000 additional common shares if its closing stock price reaches $90 for 30 consecutive days before December 2027. At December 31, 2025, the price is $62 and has never exceeded $72. Evaluate how these shares affect the 2025 annual EPS computations under ASC 260-10-45-52 through 55.', '["Include 40,000 in basic EPS because the agreement is a firm commitment", "Exclude from basic but include in diluted EPS because future fulfillment remains possible", "Exclude from basic EPS; for diluted EPS, test whether the condition would be satisfied at the reporting date — since $62 fails the $90 threshold, exclude from diluted as well", "Include a probability-weighted share count in diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-53 through 55, contingently issuable shares tied to a market-price condition are included in diluted EPS only if the condition is met at the reporting date (treating the period-end as the end of the contingency period). Because the $62 price fails the $90 target, the 40,000 shares are excluded from both basic and diluted EPS. Choice B incorrectly includes them in diluted based on the possibility of future fulfillment rather than the current-period-end test.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Two-class method: losses not allocated to non-obligated participating preferred
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A company''s Series A preferred shares participate equally in common dividends after a stated annual preference, but the certificate does not obligate Series A holders to absorb entity losses. In a year when the company reports a net loss and no dividends are declared, the controller allocates a proportional share of the loss to the Series A under the two-class method. Under ASC 260-10-45-63, is this allocation proper?', '["Yes — all equity holders share equally in profits and losses", "No — losses are allocated to a participating security only when its terms contractually require absorption of losses; absent such an obligation, the full loss belongs to common shareholders", "Yes — participation in dividends automatically implies participation in losses", "No — but a footnote disclosing the hypothetical allocation is required"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 260-10-45-63 specifies that net losses are not allocated to participating securities unless the holders are contractually obligated to share in the entity''s losses (e.g., through a deficit-funding provision). Standard participating preferred typically shares only in profits. Without a loss-absorption obligation, the entire net loss is attributed to common shareholders. Choice C incorrectly equates dividend participation with loss sharing.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — After a 1-for-5 reverse split, prior-period EPS shares are restated
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On August 1, 2025, an entity completes a 1-for-5 reverse stock split, reducing shares outstanding from 1,000,000 to 200,000. When the entity presents comparative 2024 EPS (which originally used 1,000,000 weighted-average shares), what share count appears in the 2024 column?', '["1,000,000 — the historical count is preserved", "200,000 — retroactively restated for the reverse split", "600,000 — weighted for the five months pre-split and seven months post-split", "400,000 — reflecting a partial retroactive adjustment"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, reverse stock splits — like forward splits and stock dividends — require retroactive adjustment of weighted-average shares for all periods presented. The 2024 column is restated to 200,000 (1,000,000 / 5) to ensure comparability. Choice A would mislead users by implying a 5x improvement in per-share earnings that is merely a structural change.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Reverse treasury stock method: dilutive written put with strike above market
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity sold put options requiring it to repurchase 20,000 of its shares at $75 each if holders exercise. Throughout 2025, the entity''s average share price was $50. Walk through the reverse treasury stock method to determine the net share effect for diluted EPS.', '["Step 1: Assume the entity pays $75 x 20,000 = $1,500,000 to repurchase 20,000 shares. Step 2: The entity hypothetically uses $1,500,000 to buy shares at $50, acquiring 30,000. Net effect: 30,000 - 20,000 = 10,000 additional shares in the denominator.", "No dilution — written puts are excluded when the strike exceeds market price", "30,000 additional shares — the full hypothetical repurchase is added without netting", "20,000 additional shares — the put obligation count is added directly"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, a written put with a strike price ($75) above the average market price ($50) is dilutive. The reverse treasury stock method: (1) assumes repurchase of 20,000 shares at $75 = $1,500,000; (2) assumes that cash buys back shares at $50 market = 30,000 hypothetical shares. Net increase to the denominator = 30,000 - 20,000 = 10,000 shares. Choice B incorrectly applies the "out of the money" logic from purchased options; for written puts, dilution occurs when the strike exceeds the market.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Net-share-settled convertible debt: only the conversion spread enters diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASU 2020-06, an entity has $10,000,000 of convertible notes requiring cash settlement for the principal and share delivery only for the conversion value exceeding the principal. Each $1,000 note converts at a rate equivalent to 15 shares. The average 2025 market price is $90. Evaluate the diluted EPS share impact.', '["150,000 shares are added to the denominator (the full conversion), plus an after-tax interest add-back to the numerator", "Only the in-the-money share increment enters the denominator: for each $1,000 bond, 15 shares x $90 = $1,350 conversion value; spread above $1,000 = $350, settled in $350/$90 = 3.89 shares per bond; total = 38,889 shares. No interest add-back because the principal remains a cash obligation.", "Exclude entirely because cash-settled principal means no dilution exists", "75,000 shares — half the conversion shares because only the spread is settled in stock"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-44 (post-ASU 2020-06), when the principal is mandatorily settled in cash and only the conversion spread is share-settled, diluted EPS includes only the incremental shares from the spread. Per $1,000 bond: conversion value = 15 x $90 = $1,350; spread = $350; shares = $350/$90 = 3.89. For 10,000 bonds: 38,889 shares. No numerator adjustment is needed because the interest expense relates to the cash-settled principal. Choice A incorrectly applies the full if-converted method designed for instruments entirely settleable in shares.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Warrants issued to a customer reduce transaction price and affect EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'To secure a five-year supply contract, an entity grants a customer warrants to purchase 50,000 common shares at $10 each. Under ASC 606-10-32-25, the fair value of the warrants ($200,000) reduces the transaction price allocated to the supply contract over its term. For diluted EPS, the entity''s controller excludes the warrants from the treasury stock method, arguing they are a customer incentive rather than a capital market instrument. Assess this position under ASC 260.', '["Correct — warrants issued to customers are not potential common shares", "Incorrect — regardless of why warrants were issued, they represent potential common shares; the treasury stock method must be applied using the $10 exercise price against the average market price to determine the incremental dilutive share effect", "Correct — only warrants issued to investors or employees are included in diluted EPS", "Incorrect — the warrants are included, but the $200,000 fair value is added to the numerator rather than processed through the treasury stock method"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, all instruments that entitle the holder to obtain common shares — including warrants issued to customers — are potential common shares for diluted EPS. The treasury stock method applies: assumed proceeds = $10 x 50,000 = $500,000; hypothetical shares repurchased at the average market price offset part of the 50,000 shares. The reason for issuance (revenue incentive vs. capital raise) does not exclude an instrument from the EPS computation. Choice A incorrectly carves out customer-issued warrants from the standard''s scope.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Sequential test: security becomes antidilutive in aggregate
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity''s basic EPS is $5.00. After including Security X (incremental EPS $0.30), cumulative diluted EPS falls to $4.60. After then including Security Y (incremental EPS $1.20), cumulative diluted EPS rises to $4.70. The entity also has Security Z (incremental EPS $2.00). Determine the correct diluted EPS figure to report.', '["$4.70 — all securities through Y are included", "$4.60 — Security X is included; Security Y is excluded because it raised per-share earnings from $4.60 to $4.70, and all subsequent securities are also excluded", "$5.00 — the entire computation is abandoned because the sequential test broke down", "$4.30 — include all three securities regardless of the cumulative effect"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-17 and 45-21, once a security causes the cumulative diluted EPS to increase (i.e., it is antidilutive in aggregate), that security and all remaining securities with higher incremental EPS are excluded. Including Security Y raised EPS from $4.60 to $4.70, so Y is excluded. Security Z ($2.00 incremental) is even less dilutive and is also excluded. Diluted EPS is $4.60. Choice A incorrectly retains Y despite its antidilutive aggregate effect.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Unvested nonparticipating restricted shares excluded from basic denominator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity has 500,000 total shares issued. Of these, 30,000 are unvested restricted shares with a service condition and no dividend rights until vesting. How many shares enter the basic EPS denominator?', '["500,000 — all issued shares are counted", "470,000 — unvested nonparticipating restricted shares are excluded because they are contingently returnable", "485,000 — half of the restricted shares are included as a probability adjustment", "500,000 — but with a footnote disclosing the restricted share arrangement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-14 and 45-48, unvested restricted shares that do not participate in undistributed earnings are treated as contingently returnable and excluded from the basic EPS denominator. Only the 470,000 vested (or unrestricted) shares are included. Choice A ignores the distinction between legally issued shares and shares that are contingently returnable due to forfeiture risk.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Shares issued in a business combination: weighted from acquisition date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On July 1, 2025, an acquirer issues 80,000 new common shares to complete a business combination. The acquirer had 320,000 shares from January 1 through June 30. An analyst includes 80,000 shares in the weighted average for the full year, reasoning that the acquired business generated earnings all year. Under ASC 260 and ASC 805, how should the acquisition shares be weighted?', '["Full year — the acquired entity''s earnings are included for the entire reporting period", "Six months — shares issued in a business combination enter the weighted average from the acquisition date (July 1), contributing 80,000 x 6/12 = 40,000 to the annual denominator", "Not at all — shares from business combinations are excluded from EPS until the following fiscal year", "Full year — but only if the acquirer restates the pre-combination period as if the entities had always been combined"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10 and ASC 805-10-25, consideration shares issued in a business combination enter the acquirer''s weighted-average denominator from the acquisition date. The acquired entity''s earnings join the consolidated income statement only from that date forward, so the shares and income are matched. Including the shares for the full year (Choice A) without full-year income would depress EPS and misrepresent per-share performance. Weighted average: 320,000 + (80,000 x 6/12) = 360,000.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Mandatorily convertible preferred: included in diluted EPS even when antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity issues preferred shares that automatically convert into 25,000 common shares on a fixed date two years from issuance. The annual preferred dividend is $125,000. Applying the if-converted method adds back $125,000 to the numerator and 25,000 to the denominator, raising per-share earnings above basic EPS. Should the entity exclude this instrument from diluted EPS as antidilutive?', '["Yes — the antidilution rule under ASC 260-10-45-17 applies to all instruments without exception", "No — ASC 260-10-45-36 requires mandatorily convertible securities to be included in diluted EPS from issuance regardless of whether the effect is individually antidilutive", "Yes — but only if the conversion date is more than one year away", "No — but only the share increase is included; the dividend add-back is excluded to preserve dilution"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 260-10-45-36 carves out an exception: mandatorily convertible securities bypass the standard antidilution test and are always included in diluted EPS from their issuance date. Because conversion is certain, excluding them would misstate future per-share economics. Choice A incorrectly applies the general antidilution rule, which ASC 260 specifically overrides for mandatory instruments.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Earnings-based contingency: target unmet at year-end excludes shares from both
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An earn-out provision obligates an acquirer to issue 60,000 shares if the acquired subsidiary''s EBITDA exceeds $5 million in any of the three years following acquisition. In year one, subsidiary EBITDA is $3.8 million. Management believes the target will likely be met in year two based on pipeline contracts. For year-one EPS, determine the correct treatment of the 60,000 contingent shares.', '["Include in diluted EPS because management believes the target will be met", "Include in both basic and diluted EPS at 20,000 shares (one-third of the total for one year)", "Exclude from both basic and diluted EPS — the earnings condition is not met at the year-one reporting date, so the shares fail the period-end test under ASC 260-10-45-52", "Include in diluted EPS at 60,000 shares because the condition could be satisfied in a future period"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-52 through 55, contingently issuable shares based on an earnings threshold are tested by asking whether the condition is satisfied at the reporting date (treating the reporting date as the end of the contingency period). Year-one EBITDA of $3.8M does not meet the $5M target, so the shares are excluded from both basic and diluted EPS. Management''s forward-looking belief is irrelevant; only actual results at the reporting date govern inclusion. Choice A improperly substitutes judgment for the objective period-end test.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Per-share amounts: continuing ops and net income required on the face
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A public entity with both continuing and discontinued operations prepares its annual income statement. Which per-share line items does ASC 260-10-45-3 require on the face of the income statement rather than in the notes?', '["Net income per share only", "Income from continuing operations per share and net income per share", "All individual income statement components on a per-share basis", "Diluted EPS for net income only; basic EPS may appear in the notes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 260-10-45-3 mandates that per-share amounts for income from continuing operations and net income appear on the face of the income statement. Per-share data for other items — such as discontinued operations — may be presented either on the face or in the notes, at the entity''s option. Choice A omits the continuing-operations requirement. Choice C overstates the face-of-statement mandate.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Performance-vesting options: excluded from diluted EPS when target unmet
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity''s 2025 compensation plan includes 25,000 options that vest only upon achieving an internal return-on-equity target of 15%. Actual 2025 ROE is 11%, and management assesses achievement as "reasonably possible" but not "probable." The options have a $40 exercise price and the average market price is $65. Under ASC 260, determine the diluted EPS treatment for 2025.', '["Apply the treasury stock method because the options are in the money", "Exclude entirely — the performance condition is evaluated at the reporting date, and because the 15% ROE target is not met at December 31, the options are excluded from diluted EPS regardless of moneyness", "Include at a probability-weighted amount reflecting the ''reasonably possible'' assessment", "Include because all in-the-money options must be reflected in diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, options with performance conditions enter diluted EPS only when the performance target is satisfied at the reporting date (or would be satisfied if the reporting date were the end of the contingency period). The 11% ROE does not meet the 15% threshold, so the options are excluded even though they are in the money. Moneyness is necessary but not sufficient when a performance gate exists. Choice A skips the performance-condition test entirely.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Open-market repurchase mid-year: remove shares from buyback date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Between January and August, an entity maintained 600,000 shares outstanding. On September 1, the board completed an open-market buyback of 90,000 shares. No other share activity occurred. The controller uses a full-year denominator of 510,000 (600,000 minus 90,000). Identify the error and compute the correct weighted-average.', '["The controller is correct — repurchased shares are removed for the entire year", "The weighted-average should be 570,000: the 90,000 shares are removed from September 1 only, so 600,000 x 8/12 plus 510,000 x 4/12", "The weighted-average should be 555,000: the shares are removed from the authorization date, which preceded September 1", "The repurchased shares remain in the denominator because they were not formally retired"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares leave the weighted average from the repurchase date. January through August (8 months): 600,000. September through December (4 months): 510,000. Weighted average: (600,000 x 8/12) + (510,000 x 4/12) = 400,000 + 170,000 = 570,000. The controller''s error is treating the repurchase as if it occurred on January 1, understating the denominator by 60,000 shares. Choice D is incorrect because treasury shares exit the count upon repurchase regardless of formal retirement.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — If-converted cumulative preferred: must add back dividend that was already deducted
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity has cumulative convertible preferred stock. No preferred dividends were declared during the year, yet the full annual entitlement was deducted from the basic EPS numerator. When testing the if-converted method for diluted EPS, the analyst adds back $0 to the numerator, reasoning that no dividends were actually paid. Assess this approach.', '["Correct — only cash dividends paid require add-back", "Incorrect — cumulative preferred dividends are deducted from basic EPS whether or not declared; the if-converted method must reverse that deduction by adding the full annual entitlement back to the diluted EPS numerator", "Correct — undeclared dividends are hypothetical and should not affect the if-converted test", "Incorrect — but only half the annual amount is added back for undeclared dividends"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, cumulative preferred dividends reduce the basic EPS numerator regardless of declaration. When applying the if-converted method (ASC 260-10-45-40), conversion eliminates the preferred claim, so the previously deducted amount must be added back to test dilution accurately. Failing to restore the deduction understates the if-converted numerator and distorts the dilution analysis. Choice A confuses the treatment of noncumulative preferred (declared only) with cumulative preferred.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Shares issued to settle a liability: weighted from the settlement date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On November 1, 2025, an entity settles a legal claim by issuing 36,000 common shares to the plaintiff. The entity had 400,000 shares from January 1 through October 31. What is the year-end weighted-average share count for basic EPS?', '["436,000 — the settlement shares are counted for the full year", "406,000 — the 36,000 shares are weighted for two months (November-December): 36,000 x 2/12 = 6,000", "400,000 — shares issued in non-cash transactions are excluded from the weighted average", "418,000 — the 36,000 shares are weighted for six months to reflect the midpoint of the dispute"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, new shares enter the weighted average from their issuance date regardless of the form of consideration. The 36,000 shares are weighted for November and December: 36,000 x 2/12 = 6,000. Total weighted average = 400,000 + 6,000 = 406,000. Choice C incorrectly excludes shares issued for non-cash consideration; the standard does not distinguish between cash and non-cash issuances for weighting purposes.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Per-share amounts for OCI are not required
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity''s 2025 comprehensive income statement shows $4.5 million of net income and $300,000 of unrealized gains on available-for-sale securities within other comprehensive income. A new staff accountant prepares an EPS schedule that includes a per-share line for OCI. Under ASC 260, is this per-share OCI line required?', '["Yes — each component of comprehensive income needs a per-share disclosure", "Yes — but only when OCI is material relative to net income", "No — ASC 260 mandates per-share data only for income from continuing operations and net income; OCI per-share figures are neither required nor prohibited", "No — OCI per-share data is explicitly prohibited to avoid confusion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 260-10-45-3 limits the mandatory per-share presentation to income from continuing operations and net income. While the standard does not prohibit voluntary per-share OCI disclosure, it does not require it. Choice A overstates the standard''s scope by extending the EPS requirement to every comprehensive income component. Choice D incorrectly states a prohibition — ASC 260 is silent on OCI per-share, neither requiring nor forbidding it.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Nonpublic entities electing to present EPS must follow all of ASC 260
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A family-owned manufacturing company with no publicly traded securities voluntarily includes EPS figures in its audited financial statements to satisfy a bank covenant. The CFO proposes computing EPS using a simplified average rather than the time-weighted methodology in ASC 260. Is this permissible?', '["Yes — nonpublic entities have flexibility to use any reasonable EPS methodology", "No — ASC 260-10-15-2 requires any entity that elects to present EPS to comply with every provision of the standard, including the weighted-average methodology", "Yes — bank covenant disclosures are exempt from GAAP presentation requirements", "No — but the entity may present only basic EPS and omit diluted"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is optional for nonpublic entities, but once an entity elects to report EPS, it must follow ASC 260 in its entirety. This includes using the weighted-average share methodology, presenting both basic and diluted figures, and applying all dilution tests. Choice A incorrectly grants nonpublic entities latitude to deviate from the standard they have voluntarily adopted.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Parent shares held by consolidated subsidiary are treasury shares for EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A parent company owns 90% of a subsidiary. The subsidiary purchased 20,000 of the parent''s common shares on the open market as a long-term investment. The parent has 800,000 total shares issued. When preparing consolidated EPS, the parent''s controller includes all 800,000 shares in the denominator, arguing the subsidiary is a separate legal entity. Evaluate this position.', '["Correct — the subsidiary''s legal-entity status means its investments are not reclassified in consolidation", "Incorrect — in consolidation, parent shares held by any consolidated subsidiary are reclassified as treasury stock and removed from shares outstanding; the denominator should be 780,000", "Correct — but a footnote disclosing the subsidiary''s holding is required", "Incorrect — only the parent''s proportionate share (90% of 20,000 = 18,000) should be excluded"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under consolidation principles and ASC 260-10-45-10, parent shares held by a consolidated subsidiary are treated as treasury shares in the consolidated financial statements. All 20,000 shares are excluded from the denominator regardless of the parent''s ownership percentage in the subsidiary. Choice D incorrectly prorates by ownership; the elimination applies to all parent shares the subsidiary holds. Choice A ignores that consolidation collapses legal-entity boundaries.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Error restatement requires recalculating all prior-period EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'In 2025, an entity discovers it overstated 2023 revenue by $400,000 due to a cutoff error, requiring retrospective restatement under ASC 250. The entity''s 2025 comparative income statement includes a restated 2023 column. Must the per-share amounts in the 2023 column also be recalculated?', '["No — EPS figures from previously issued statements remain unchanged", "Yes — ASC 250 and ASC 260-10-45-2 require that restated financial data include correspondingly restated per-share amounts", "Yes — but only diluted EPS is restated; basic EPS is unchanged", "No — the EPS effect is disclosed only in the restatement footnote"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-1 and ASC 260-10-45-2, retrospective restatement of prior-period financial statements extends to all per-share data. The 2023 EPS figures must be recalculated using the corrected income. Choice A incorrectly treats previously reported EPS as permanent; the purpose of restatement is to present corrected information as if the error had never occurred, including per-share measures.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — DRIP shares enter weighted average from each issuance date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Through a dividend reinvestment plan (DRIP), an entity issues new shares to participating shareholders each quarter. In 2025, DRIP issuances were: 2,000 shares on March 31, 2,000 on June 30, 2,000 on September 30, and 2,000 on December 31. The entity had 300,000 shares on January 1 before any DRIP activity. Under ASC 260, how are DRIP-issued shares reflected in the weighted average?', '["All 8,000 DRIP shares are included for the full year", "Each quarterly tranche is weighted from its issuance date: March shares for 9/12, June for 6/12, September for 3/12, and December for 0/12", "DRIP shares are excluded because they replace cash dividends rather than raise new capital", "DRIP shares are weighted from the ex-dividend date, which precedes each issuance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares enter the weighted average from their issuance date regardless of how or why they were issued. DRIP shares are no exception. The March tranche contributes 2,000 x 9/12 = 1,500; June: 2,000 x 6/12 = 1,000; September: 2,000 x 3/12 = 500; December: 2,000 x 0/12 = 0. Total DRIP contribution: 3,000. Weighted average: 303,000. Choice A incorrectly treats all DRIP shares as outstanding for the entire year.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Year-to-date interim EPS: recompute from scratch, not sum of quarters
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity reports quarterly basic EPS of $1.20 in Q1, $0.80 in Q2, $1.50 in Q3, and $0.90 in Q4. A financial analyst sums these to derive full-year basic EPS of $4.40. Under ASC 260-10-45-2 and ASC 270, is this summation approach correct?', '["Yes — annual EPS is the sum of the four quarterly EPS figures", "No — full-year basic EPS must be independently computed using cumulative annual income divided by the cumulative annual weighted-average shares; the sum of quarterly EPS will not equal annual EPS when share counts change between quarters", "Yes — but only when the number of shares outstanding is unchanged across all four quarters", "No — annual EPS equals the simple average of quarterly EPS: ($1.20 + $0.80 + $1.50 + $0.90) / 4"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, annual EPS is computed using full-year income divided by the full-year weighted-average share count. Quarterly EPS figures use quarterly weighted averages that may differ due to mid-year issuances or repurchases. Summing quarterly EPS double-counts or under-counts the effect of share changes and does not yield the correct annual figure. Choice C identifies the only scenario where summation works but does not make the method correct in general.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Entity-option settlement contract: presume share settlement if dilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity may settle a $600,000 obligation by either paying cash or delivering 12,000 of its common shares at its election. The average market price is $70. Management states it intends to pay cash. Under ASC 260-10-45-45, how does this contract affect diluted EPS?', '["No effect — management''s intent to settle in cash controls the EPS treatment", "Presumed share settlement: add 12,000 shares to the denominator and reduce the numerator by $600,000 — but only if this produces a dilutive result", "Presumed share settlement: add 12,000 shares to the denominator with no numerator adjustment — because share settlement eliminates the cash outflow, and include only if dilutive", "The contract is excluded because it is a liability, not an equity instrument"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-45, contracts settleable in stock or cash at the entity''s option are presumed settled in shares if the effect is dilutive. Share settlement adds 12,000 shares to the denominator. No numerator adjustment is needed because the $600,000 obligation would be extinguished by share delivery rather than cash, so income is unaffected. The test: $1,200,000 / 312,000 = lower than basic? If yes, include. Choice A incorrectly defers to management intent; diluted EPS assumes the most dilutive settlement.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Weighted-average with no share changes equals period-end shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity maintained exactly 200,000 common shares throughout all of 2025 with no issuances, repurchases, dividends, or splits. Its net income was $800,000 with no preferred stock. What is the weighted-average share count for basic EPS?', '["200,000", "100,000 — the simple average of beginning and ending shares", "200,000 — but a monthly reconciliation must be disclosed in the notes", "Cannot be determined without monthly outstanding share data"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted average equals the number of shares outstanding when no changes occur during the period. With 200,000 shares for all 12 months, the weighted average is simply 200,000. Basic EPS = $800,000 / 200,000 = $4.00. Choice B incorrectly applies an averaging method that has no basis in the standard. Choice D is incorrect because constant share counts need no monthly data.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q27: easy / L2 — When cumulative preferred exceeds net income, basic EPS is negative
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity reports net income of $50,000 and has 100,000 weighted-average common shares. Cumulative preferred dividends of $90,000 were earned by preferred holders during the year (none declared). A junior analyst states that basic EPS cannot be negative when the entity is profitable. Is this correct?', '["Yes — basic EPS is floored at zero when net income is positive", "No — income available to common shareholders is $50,000 minus $90,000 = negative $40,000, yielding basic EPS of $(0.40); a profitable entity can report negative EPS when preferred claims exceed earnings", "Yes — undeclared cumulative dividends are only disclosed, not deducted", "No — but the entity should present $0.00 and disclose the negative amount in the notes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, cumulative preferred dividends are deducted from net income to compute income available to common shareholders whether or not declared. Here: $50,000 - $90,000 = $(40,000). Basic EPS = $(40,000) / 100,000 = $(0.40). There is no floor at zero. Choice A incorrectly assumes profitability guarantees positive per-share results; preferred claims can consume more than the entity''s earnings.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q28: hard / L3 — Average market price for TSM must be period average, not closing price
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity has 30,000 outstanding stock options with a $25 exercise price. The average market price during 2025 was $40, but the December 31 closing price was $55. A staff accountant applies the treasury stock method using the $55 closing price, computing 30,000 - (30,000 x $25 / $55) = 16,364 incremental shares. The senior reviewer rejects this. Under ASC 260-10-45-28, what price should be used and what is the correct incremental share count?', '["The $55 year-end closing price, yielding 16,364 incremental shares — the most current price best reflects dilution", "The $40 period average price, yielding 11,250 incremental shares — the treasury stock method requires the average market price during the period, not the closing price", "The higher of the average or closing price to maximize reported dilution", "The $25 exercise price, yielding 0 incremental shares — assumed proceeds offset all shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28, the treasury stock method uses the average market price during the period for the hypothetical share repurchase. Using $40: assumed proceeds = 30,000 x $25 = $750,000; hypothetical repurchase = $750,000 / $40 = 18,750 shares; incremental = 30,000 - 18,750 = 11,250. The closing price would overstate dilution in rising markets and understate it in falling markets. Choice A uses the wrong price, producing an inflated incremental share count.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Voluntary accounting-principle change: restate prior-period EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'In 2025, an entity voluntarily changes its depreciation method from double-declining balance to straight-line, applied retrospectively under ASC 250. The change increases 2024 net income by $180,000. When presenting the 2024 comparative column in the 2025 financial statements, must the entity recalculate 2024 EPS?', '["No — EPS is restated only for error corrections, not voluntary accounting changes", "Yes — retrospective application under ASC 250 extends to all comparative financial data, including per-share amounts; 2024 EPS must reflect the straight-line income", "Yes — but only diluted EPS changes; basic EPS remains as originally reported", "No — the cumulative effect is reported in 2025 retained earnings with no EPS impact on prior periods"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-5 and ASC 260-10-45-2, retrospective application of a voluntary accounting change requires restating all prior-period financial statements presented for comparability, including EPS. The 2024 EPS must be recalculated using the straight-line net income. Choice A incorrectly limits EPS restatement to error corrections; the restatement principle applies equally to retrospective accounting changes.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Out-of-the-money written calls: no dilutive effect
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity wrote (sold) call options allowing holders to buy 40,000 of its shares at $80 each. Throughout 2025, the entity''s average share price was $55. Should these written calls appear in the diluted EPS computation?', '["Yes — written instruments on the entity''s own stock always affect diluted EPS", "Yes — the reverse treasury stock method applies to all written calls", "No — the $80 exercise price exceeds the $55 average market price, making the calls out of the money and therefore antidilutive under the treasury stock method", "No — written calls are categorically excluded from EPS because they benefit the entity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22 and the treasury stock method, call options (whether purchased or written) are dilutive only when the exercise price is below the average market price. With an $80 strike versus $55 market, the calls are out of the money — no rational holder would exercise. They are antidilutive and excluded. Choice D is incorrect because written calls that are in the money (strike below market) would be dilutive and must be included.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
